"David Gale" <dsgSPAMFILTER@alum.dartmouth.org> writes:
> Ok, this one may need some source-diving to answer.  Be warned.  :-)
> 
> I've seen in several spoilers comments along the lines of "having too many
> leather items, for instance, in a pile when using a wand of polymorph will
> cause it to turn into a golem, so use multiple smaller piles."  Which leaves
> me wondering: what are the safe limits for poly-piling?  That is, how many
> items of a given material can be in one pile without risking it being
> golem-ized? 

>From wan2-342.txt:

  You will eventually lose some objects.  Items appearing in quantities of
  2 or more have a (quantity in 1000) chance of merging into one object.
  Items also have a probability of "shuddering": 1/3 if a wand or cursed,
  1/8 if uncursed, 1/12 if blessed (if in quantities greater than 4, 100%,
  1/4, and 1/6, respectively).  This will decrease the quantity of the item
  randomly, cause any remainder not to be polymorphed, and has a one in
  (luck + 45) chance of creating a golem from the material of the shuddered
  item. Scrolls of mail will never merge or shudder.

True as far as it goes, but incomplete: there must be at least two
items (stacked or separate) remaining in the pile (not necessarily of
the golem's material). I'll add a note to that effect. This means that
there must be at least three items total in a pile to start with for
there to be a chance of a golem at all (since any lost by shuddering
don't count against that limit).

> Assuming that it's some sort of progression, when does the
> golem percentage go above 25%?  50%?

This is hugely dependent on what you're putting into the stacks and in
what quantities; see the formulae above and work out your own figures :-)

